# Meridian Crossing — environment template
#
#   cp .env.example .env.local     then uncomment only the lines you need
#
# `.env.local` is gitignored and is where your real keys go. This file is the committed template: it
# lists EVERY variable the code reads, with its default. Every line is commented out on purpose — a
# freshly copied `.env.local` changes nothing, so `pnpm demo` still runs deterministically, offline,
# on one host with no keys at all.
#
# Loaded automatically by `pnpm demo`, `pnpm suppliers`, `pnpm sample` and `pnpm sweep` (see
# infra/env.mjs). NOT loaded by `pnpm test`, which stays hermetic and offline by design.
#
# The real environment wins over this file, so a one-off still works:
#
#   NEGOTIATION_SEED=rehearsal pnpm demo --web
#
# Format notes: no `export` keyword, no shell expansion. An unquoted `#` starts a comment mid-line, so
# quote any value containing one — LLM_API_KEY="sk-or-v1-…#…".


# ── Reasoning ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# With LLM_BASE_URL unset, all five agents use their deterministic reasoners: fully offline, same
# outcome every run. Set it and all five reason through any OpenAI-compatible gateway.

#LLM_BASE_URL=https://openrouter.ai/api/v1
#LLM_API_KEY=sk-...
# Any tool-calling model. deepseek/deepseek-v3.2 is the built-in default but has been seen rate-limited
# on OpenRouter's shared pool; anthropic/claude-haiku-4.5 is the safest pick for a live demo.
#LLM_MODEL=deepseek/deepseek-v3.2

# Per-agent override; wins over LLM_MODEL.
#BUYER_LLM_MODEL=
#SUMMIT_LLM_MODEL=
#CASCADE_LLM_MODEL=
#ALPINE_LLM_MODEL=
#RIDGE_LLM_MODEL=

# Pins each seller's private disposition (deal hunger, outside option, time pressure) so a run
# reproduces. Default: the negotiationId, which varies per run. Pins the sellers' circumstances only —
# with a gateway configured the model still varies.
#NEGOTIATION_SEED=


# ── Pacing & oversight ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Pace of the buyer's turns — the audience's read speed, and the kill switch's live window.
# `pnpm demo` picks a per-mode DEFAULT: 2000 with --web (a human is reading), 0 for the terminal run
# (nobody is). Setting it here overrides both, for every mode. 0 for any unattended run.
#TURN_DELAY_MS=2000
# How long a held deal waits for a human before parking.
#APPROVAL_TIMEOUT_MS=600000


# ── Dashboard & control surface ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# The only port that ever needs exposing.
#DASHBOARD_PORT=41200
# HTTP Basic Auth for the dashboard. Unset DASHBOARD_PASS ⇒ open, with a startup warning. Set it before
# publishing the port on any shared host.
#DASHBOARD_USER=operator
#DASHBOARD_PASS=

# Shared secret for EVERY buyer route: the state-changing ones (kill / approve / settle, plus the three
# settlement actions, which fail closed with no token set) and the six gated reads — /audit, /record,
# /settlement, /state, /approvals, and the buyer's own /events. `pnpm demo` auto-provisions one per run
# for --web/--usdc and hands the same value to the buyer and the dashboard proxy, so you only set this
# to pin it.
#CONTROL_TOKEN=


# ── Settlement (--usdc) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Stripe TEST secret key. Required by --usdc; without it the settlement layer never mounts and the run
# continues in the mode you launched, minus settlement (no USDC, no human-approval step), logging a
# startup warning first. --usdc does not imply --web: a keyless terminal run stays a terminal run.
# Stripe Dashboard → Developers → API keys, test mode.
#STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=sk_test_...

# Deal total above which payment waits for a human (in --web). The deterministic deal is $9,168, so the
# default puts the approval button on the default path.
#SETTLEMENT_APPROVAL_ABOVE_USD=9100
# How long to poll Stripe for on-chain capture before leaving the settle in DEPOSIT_SENT.
#SETTLEMENT_CAPTURE_TIMEOUT_MS=30000
# How often to poll within that budget.
#SETTLEMENT_POLL_INTERVAL_MS=1500

# Standard proxy vars — when EITHER SPELLING is set (the code reads uppercase HTTPS_PROXY and lowercase
# https_proxy), the Stripe SDK is built on the fetch HTTP client so it routes through the proxy. Usually
# inherited from the shell rather than set here.
#HTTPS_PROXY=
#https_proxy=

# Global fetch only reads those proxy vars where Node's proxy-env support is both PRESENT (22.21+/24.5+)
# and ENABLED, because it is opt-in even where it exists. A --usdc settle with HTTPS_PROXY set and
# neither condition met refuses, rather than sending the Stripe secret key out unproxied — the branch
# used to select the fetch client on the engines range alone and leak quietly on a Node without the flag.
# Node reads both of these WHEN IT STARTS, so set them in your shell: from this file they arrive too late
# to have any effect.
#NODE_USE_ENV_PROXY=1
#NODE_OPTIONS=--use-env-proxy


# ── Discovery, transport & wire format ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# a2cn swaps in the open-standard codec at the message boundary.
#WIRE_PROFILE=meridian
# The AGNTCY Agent Directory's gRPC address.
#DIR_ADDRESS=localhost:8888
# What the agntcy-dir SDK itself reads; mirrors DIR_ADDRESS. Set only to diverge from it.
#DIRECTORY_CLIENT_SERVER_ADDRESS=

# NOTE: SLIM_IMAGE_REF (required by infra/slim/docker-compose.yml, which refuses to float to :latest)
# is read by Docker Compose, not by Node — it does NOT come from this file. Export it in your shell, or
# put it in infra/slim/.env, which Compose reads itself. See infra/VERSIONS.md.


# ── Ports & hosts ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Per-supplier A2A port. Validated — a non-integer is a hard error, not a silent NaN.
#SUMMIT_PORT=41001
#ALPINE_PORT=41002
#RIDGE_PORT=41003
#CASCADE_PORT=41004

# Point an agent somewhere else entirely. Default: http://localhost:<its port>.
#SUMMIT_URL=
#ALPINE_URL=
#RIDGE_URL=
#CASCADE_URL=

# The buyer's control + SSE server.
#BUYER_HTTP_PORT=41100
# Where the dashboard's reverse proxy looks for the agents.
#AGENT_HOST=127.0.0.1

# Interface the agents listen on. Set 0.0.0.0 only for a multi-host deployment.
#A2A_BIND_HOST=127.0.0.1
# Comma-separated allowlist of permitted agent origins. Required for any non-loopback endpoint learned
# from discovery — a capability record is authored by the counterparty, so its a2aEndpoint is untrusted
# input. Unset ⇒ loopback-only, and a non-loopback origin fails loudly naming this variable.
#A2A_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=http://summit.internal,https://alpine.example


# ── Observability ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# 1 turns on tracing: real OTel spans, one per negotiation, carrying AGNTCY-STYLE `agntcy.*`
# attributes. That is a naming convention, NOT the published AGNTCY observability schema — nothing
# here emits a GenAI semantic-convention attribute.
#OTEL_ENABLED=1
# File exporter target — one JSON span per line, no collector needed.
#OTEL_TRACES_FILE=trails/otel-spans.jsonl
# When set, spans stream to a real collector instead of the file.
#OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT=http://localhost:4318
# Overrides the service name on emitted spans. Default: per-agent, e.g. supplier-cascade.
#OTEL_SERVICE_NAME=


# ── Sampling harness (pnpm sample) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Number of samples.
#N=12
# Which suppliers to negotiate. RidgeLine is excluded by default — it is rejected at the trust gate in
# the live run and never negotiates, so including it would measure a negotiation that cannot happen.
#SUPS=summit,cascade
# 1 also prints the last exchanges of the first runs.
#ENDGAME=1


# ── Set by the launcher — do not set these by hand ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# AWAIT_START             arms the flow behind the dashboard's Start button in --web
# USDC_SETTLEMENT         turns on the settlement layer for --usdc
# SETTLEMENT_AUTO_APPROVE lets terminal --usdc, which has no operator UI, approve its own
#                         over-threshold payment — deliberately NEVER set in --web, where a human owns
#                         that step
#
# infra/demo.mjs derives each from the flags you pass and injects them into the agent processes. Values
# it injects override this file, so setting them here has no effect under `pnpm demo` — and setting
# SETTLEMENT_AUTO_APPROVE would be an attempt to remove the human from the one step that has one.
#
# Starting an agent DIRECTLY, outside `pnpm demo`, is the exception: no launcher is in the way, so all
# three are yours to set — and keeping the human in the payment step becomes yours to enforce.
